    "design_critique": "1) Integral term on curves. The rationale treats ki=0.3 as a fix for a constant motor-mismatch bias, but the integral accumulates the signal. On a sustained bend the signal stays non-zero for a long time, so the integral winds up during the bend and then overshoots on exit. The +/-2 clamp bounds the size (0.6 turn, about 210 PWM) but not the duration or direction of the error. The rationale does not estimate the mismatch magnitude, so it cannot show that ki is needed. Given that the disturbance is about 2 percent, ki=0 with a small kp-based offset tolerance may be safer. Nothing in the revision shows the bias is large enough to justify the risk.

2) Speed and the PWM-to-speed claim. The rationale says real speed will fall below the PWM-based estimate, then uses that PWM-based estimate (about 90 percent PWM) to justify 1.8 as enough for under 3 s. That is internally inconsistent. The rationale gives no maximum wheel speed, battery sag figure, or start-from-rest allowance, so 'completion_time about 2.6-2.9 s' is an unsupported number.

3) Actuator saturation at high base speed. At base_speed 1.8 the base PWM is about 3685. Any turn above about 1.17 pushes the outer wheel command to the 4095 clamp, so steering authority on that side is lost while the inner wheel is still reduced. The rationale does not mention this asymmetry, which matters most on the slalom and tight bends where kp correction is largest.

